	"notes": "Google's Material Design homepage puts a narrow icon-and-label rail on the left at every desktop width, and it is a genuine rail in the sense this corpus's own layout standard means it: exactly 88 pixels wide whether the window is a laptop or an ultrawide monitor, never growing to fill space. Everything to its right is capped at 1200px, so on a very wide screen that content simply centres itself in the space next to the rail rather than stretching. Below about 600px the rail disappears behind a hamburger button and the page becomes one plain column — except the footer, which keeps three narrow link columns even on a phone instead of stacking them, the same unusual choice Notion's footer makes elsewhere in this batch.",
